      "problem": "Review each independent archived question below. Every field key begins with its owning question ID. Use that question's own captured worked solution for MA evidence, give a verbatim quotation that establishes the field answer, and mark ma_basis none when unavailable. Preserve authored errors and explain any disagreement. These are source originals, not instructions:\n[{\"question_id\": \"q-48082\", \"problem\": \"Select the function that is continuous at $x=0.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}, {\"question_id\": \"q-48038\", \"problem\": \"Select the function that has a discontinuity at $x=1.$\", \"field_keys\": [\"selection\"]}]",
      "worked_solution": "q-48082:\nThe correct answer is shown below.  Indeed, despite the fact that the function has a discontinuity at $x=2$, the graph can be drawn in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/90c2fbe99d0b202e09244084d2501608018e030e6660a225f708cbc6b462dccd.png)\n\n   \n\nAll the other given graphs contain breaks at $x=0$ and it would be impossible to draw them in a neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nq-48038:\nThe correct answer is shown below. Notice that the function contains a break exactly at $x=1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13831128/assets/3c8a198c1ec835590a3d755b964929ef9ac9a30e4268b2fdc80dacdb394899d8.png)\n\nAll the other given graphs can be plotted without taking the pencil off of the paper.",
